Single-Layer Perceptron (SLP) 
A Single-Layer Perceptron (SLP) is the simplest type of artificial neural network and consists of a single layer of neurons. The perceptron 
algorithm is a type of linear classifier, meaning it tries to separate data into different classes using a linear decision boundary. 
Structure of a Single-Layer Perceptron 
A Single-Layer Perceptron (SLP) consists of the following components: 
1. Input Layer: 
o The input layer receives the features of the dataset. For example, if you are predicting whether an email is spam or not, the 
inputs could be different features like the number of certain keywords in the email, its length, etc. 
2. Weights: 
o Each input has an associated weight, which determines the importance of that input. These weights are updated during 
training. 
3. Bias: 
o The bias term is added to the weighted sum of inputs. It helps the perceptron make decisions even when the input values 
are zero. It allows the decision boundary to be shifted. 
4. Activation Function: 
o The perceptron uses an activation function (usually a step function or threshold function) to make its decision based on the 
weighted sum of inputs. If the sum is above a certain threshold, it will activate (output 1); otherwise, it will not activate 
(output 0). 
