Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) 
A Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) is a type of artificial neural network (ANN) that consists of multiple layers of neurons, including one or 
more hidden layers between the input and output layers. Unlike a single-layer perceptron (SLP), which is limited to linearly separable 
problems, an MLP is capable of solving complex, non-linear problems. This ability to learn non-linear decision boundaries makes MLPs 
more powerful and flexible than single-layer perceptrons. 
Structure of a Multi-Layer Perceptron 
An MLP consists of the following layers: 
1. Input Layer: 
o This layer receives the input features. Each neuron in the input layer corresponds to one feature in the dataset. For 
example, if you have three features for each sample, the input layer will have three neurons. 
2. Hidden Layers: 
o These are the layers between the input and output layers. MLPs can have one or more hidden layers, each consisting of 
multiple neurons. The hidden layers enable the network to learn complex representations and patterns from the data. 
o Each neuron in a hidden layer applies a transformation (weighted sum followed by an activation function) to the inputs it 
receives from the previous layer. 
3. Output Layer: 
o The output layer produces the final prediction or classification. The number of neurons in the output layer depends on the 
task. For a binary classification task, there is typically one output neuron, while for multi-class classification, there will 
be one output neuron per class. 
The multi-layer perceptron (MLP) is a generalization of the perceptron. It consists of an input layer, one or more hidden layers, and an 
output layer. The addition of hidden layers allows the MLP to model complex, non-linear relationships and solve problems like XOR and 
other non-linearly separable tasks. 
An MLP uses non-linear activation functions such as sigmoid, ReLU, or tanh in the hidden layers, enabling it to learn non-linear decision 
boundaries. 
